<Shell title="GIS & automation" active="GIS & automation"><section class="wrap section"><small>SPATIAL ANALYSIS</small><h1>GIS & automation</h1><p class="intro">From raw observations to maps that can be checked against the source data.</p><div class="case"><figure><img src="/media/map-3.webp" alt="Sentinel-2 burn severity and VIIRS fire detections in South Carolina"/><figcaption>South Carolina · Wildfires, 2025</figcaption></figure><div><small>WORKFLOW CASE STUDY</small><h2>Mapping a burn scar</h2><p>How closely do satellite fire detections follow a mapped burn scar? This study pairs Sentinel-2 burn severity with VIIRS detections so the two observations can be inspected at the same scale.</p><p>VIIRS records active fire at satellite overpass time. Burn severity captures landscape change across an interval. Their footprints answer different questions.</p><a href="/assets/map1.pdf">Read the map (PDF) ↗</a></div></div><div class="feature"><small>AUTOMATED ANALYSIS</small><h3>Rebuilding a national hazard map</h3><p>Cascadia’s command-line workflow collects public inputs, aligns them to a common grid and renders a six-panel conditions map. Its validation commands evaluate flood and fire scores against independently observed events.</p><a href="/projects/cascadia.html">Explore the workflow →</a></div><h2>Map & visualization archive</h2><div class="cards">{gallery.map(g=><a class="card" href={g.href}><div class="thumb"><img loading="lazy" src={g.image} alt=""/></div><div class="pad"><h3>{g.title}</h3><p>{g.desc}</p></div></a>)}</div><h2>Interactive tools</h2><div class="actions"><a href="/#projects">Featured applications →</a><a href="https://shawnatwsu.github.io/HazardApp/">Launch HazardApp ↗</a></div></section></Shell>
